bullets(s,8.35,4.7,4.45,2.3,[("The weights move with the prices. ",f"Quantities are held fixed, not today's dollar shares: a 2016 project was about {IX['installed']['share_2016']:.0%} battery and comes to 2026 at {IX['installed']['factor_2016']:.2f}x; a 2018 project was {IX['installed']['share_2018']:.0%} battery, {IX['installed']['factor_2018']:.2f}x; 2021 and later about 1.0x. The 5 Oct review challenge stands: the earlier version held a 40% share fixed and made every old project dearer (median ${IX['installed_fixed_share']['median']:,.0f})."),(f"Widening the class to {RC['n']} projects, {RC['n_developer']} developer-owned, ",f"puts Hyde Park's station-direct cost at the {RC['hp_rank']['b4']:.0%} percentile nominal and {IX['installed']['hp_pct']['station_direct']:.0%} re-based; the developer sites carry no utility station, no landfill and no siting case."),(f"The battery curve you pick moves the median by {(max(IX[k]['median'] for k in ('installed','pack','turnkey','nrel'))-min(IX[k]['median'] for k in ('installed','pack','turnkey','nrel')))/AJ['nominal']['median']:.0%}; ","slides 14 to 18 show the curves and what moves it more.")],size=9.5,gap=3)
notes(s,f"Reference class redefined on 2 Oct 2026 at the reviewer's request: every project at or below 100 MWh with an all-in disclosed cost, any owner; and on 5 Oct 2026 restricted to projects verified as operating (status audit: {json.load(open(f'{B}/stats_results.json'))['status_counts']['not_built']} of {json.load(open(f'{B}/stats_results.json'))['status_counts']['total']} dataset rows were planned, under development, under construction or cancelled and are out; the pre-audit class is kept in the workbook). Within the class, {json.load(open(f'{B}/stats_results.json'))['reference_class_actuals']['n']} members have a final or actual cost on record (median ${json.load(open(f'{B}/stats_results.json'))['reference_class_actuals']['median_kwh']:,.0f}); the rest are built projects whose public figure is a contract price, approved budget or announcement. {RC['n']} members ({RC['n_utility']} utility-owned, {RC['n_developer']} developer-owned); utility-only subclass of {json.load(open(f'{B}/stats_results.json'))['reference_class_utility']['n']} kept in the workbook for continuity (median ${json.load(open(f'{B}/stats_results.json'))['reference_class_utility']['median_kwh']:,.0f}). Model (revised 5 Oct 2026 after the review challenge that 2016-2018 batteries were far dearer): fixed quantities, not fixed shares. C(y) = s0 x I(y)/I(2024) + (1 - s0) x HW(y)/HW(2024) with s0 the 2024 battery share (0.40 utility-owned, 0.55 developer-owned); cost_2026 = cost_y x C(2026)/C(y). The battery share of a project in its own year is s0 x I(y)/I(2024) / C(y): {IX['installed']['share_2016']:.0%} in 2016, {IX['installed']['share_2018']:.0%} in 2018, 40% in 2024. I = the battery index in the row (installed: EIA 2015-2022 annual figure data, LBNL 2024), HW = Handy-Whitman North Atlantic production-plant index (700 in 2015, 745 in 2018, 1,112 in 2026; PJM posting). The earlier fixed-share version (today's 40% applied to every year) gave a median of ${IX['installed_fixed_share']['median']:,.0f} and is kept in the workbook for the record. Caveat on the installed series: EIA's 2022 point ($396, a sample dominated by large four-hour hybrids) sits below LBNL's 2024 point ($458, a different population), so 2022-vintage members re-base about 15% dearer while 2021 members re-base slightly cheaper; the splice is as published and is flagged in the memo. Naive whole-cost deflation at the battery rate: ${IX['installed']['naive_median']:,.0f} (installed), ${IX['pack']['naive_median']:,.0f} (pack). Sources on the workbook tab '2026$ Adjustment' and in the research log.")
